    "created_at": "2025-09-15T14:32:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-15T14:32:20Z",
    "body": "To try and clarify this a bit further I've written out some examples, specifically focusing on label's for attribute relationships.\n\nThese will assume we add a new forElement property:\n\n```webidl\npartial interface HTMLLabelElement {\n    [CEReactions, Reflect=\"for\"] attribute Element? forElement; // Could be HTMLElement? but popoverTargetElement used Element?\n};\n```\n\nExample 1: Implicit for relationship\n\n```html\n<label id=\"implicit\">\n  Implicit Label\n  <input id=\"implicitInput\">\n</label>\n\n<script>\nimplicit.forElement == null; // There's no explicit for attr-associated element so this would return null.\nimplicit.control == implicitInput; // The implicit relationship does exist and so would work for .control.\n</script>\n```\n\nExample 2: Explicit for relationship\n\n```html\n<label id=\"explicit\" for=\"explicitInput\"> Explicit Label </label>\n<input id=\"explicitInput\">\n<script>\nexplicit.forElement == explicitInput;\nexplicit.control == explicitInput;\n</script>\n```\n\nExample 3: Invalid relationship (non-labelable target)\n\n```html\n<label id=\"invalid\" for=\"nonInput\"> Invalid Label </label>\n<div id=\"nonInput\"></div>\n<script>\ninvalid.forElement == nonInput; // Following popoverTargetElement this returns the IDREF target even though it's not actually valid currently.\ninvalid.control == null; // This property validates that the target is labelable so returns null.\n</script>\n```\n\nExample 4: Imperative Relationship\n\n```html\n<label id=\"imperative\"> Imperative Label </label>\n<input id=\"imperativeInput\">\n<script>\nimperative.forElement = imperativeInput; // This is a new ability \nimperative.forElement == imperativeInput;\nimperative.control == imperativeInput;\n</script>\n```\n\nExample 5: Valid Reference Target\n\n```html\n<label id=\"referencing\" for=\"reference\"> Reference Targeted Label </label>\n<custom-element id=\"reference\">\n  <template shadowrootmode=\"open\" shadowrootreferencetarget=\"input\">\n    <input id=\"input\">\n  </template>\n</custom-element>\n\n<script>\nreferencing.forElement == reference;\nreferencing.control == reference;\n</script>\n```\n\nNote here that control isn't actually returning the labelable element, this is why for other attributes such as \"input list\" the list property type has to be changed from `HTMLDataListElement?` to just `HTMLElement?` (or Element?).\n\nExample 6: Invalid Reference Target (where the custom element is *not* form associated)\n\n```html\n<label id=\"referencing\" for=\"reference\"> Reference Targeted Label </label>\n<custom-element id=\"reference\">\n  <template shadowrootmode=\"open\" shadowrootreferencetarget=\"nonInput\">\n    <div id=\"nonInput\"></div>\n  </template>\n</custom-element>\n\n<script>\nreferencing.forElement == reference; // Essentially the same as example 3\nreferencing.control == null; // The reference doesn't resolve to a valid labelable element so this returns null.\n</script>\n```\n\nThe big question is on the forElement behaviour in the invalid case. Domenic seems to want control and forElement to be the same here (both return null). I lean more towards the distinct behaviour.\n\nIf we do decide to go with Domenic's approach the question then becomes do we even need a new forElement? Personally I like the consistency of the element prefix. If these properties already did reflection then I'd be fine not adding new ones, but given changes in behaviour would be needed a new property makes sense to me.\n\nIt also makes feature detection a bit neater:\n\n```js\nif ('forElement' in HTMLLabelElement)\n```\n\nvs\n\n```js\nif (Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(HTMLLabelElement.prototype, 'control').set != undefined)\n```\n\nHopefully this makes things a bit clearer in the discussion?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
